<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / USB-C cable buying guide</p>
<p class="page-kicker">USB-C decision guide</p>
<h1 class="page-title">USB-C cable buying guide: choose power, data, and video separately.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">“USB-C cable” describes the connector family, not one guaranteed feature set. A cable can be excellent for charging and slow for data, fast for data but not rated for your laptop’s charging current, or fully capable while the host port itself lacks video support.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Write down what the cable has to do: maximum charging power, required data rate, whether it must carry native display video, whether the device requires USB4 or Thunderbolt, and the length. Buy a cable whose manufacturer or certification marking explicitly covers every one of those requirements. Do not infer capability from connector shape, thickness, price, or “fast charge” wording.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Power</p><h2>Use the documented power rating.</h2>
<p>USB-IF’s current certified USB-C to USB-C cable marking scheme uses power logos such as 60W and 240W. Five-amp operation above 60W requires an electronically marked cable. Older 100W-rated cables remain common in the market, so the practical rule is to verify the manufacturer’s explicit watt/current rating rather than rely on appearance.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Data</p><h2>Charging wattage does not tell you data speed.</h2>
<p>A cable marketed for high charging power can carry only USB 2.0 data. Conversely, a high-speed cable can have a lower charging rating. Check the stated data rate and USB generation/certification separately from power.</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Charging only / basic data</strong><p>USB 2.0 data can be enough for charging, keyboards, and simple peripherals.</p></div>
<div><strong>External SSD / dock data</strong><p>Choose the data rate required by the device and host. The slowest link in the chain sets the ceiling.</p></div>
<div><strong>USB4 / Thunderbolt</strong><p>Use a cable explicitly certified/documented for the required USB4 or Thunderbolt mode and supported length.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Video</p><h2>The cable and the host both need the right path.</h2>
<p>Native DisplayPort Alt Mode uses USB-C high-speed lanes, so a charging/USB-2-only cable is not a substitute for a cable documented for video-capable high-speed operation. But replacing the cable cannot add DisplayPort Alt Mode to a host port that does not support it.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Length</p><h2>Do not assume every feature survives at every passive-cable length.</h2>
<p>High data rates become harder to maintain as passive cable length increases. For USB4, Thunderbolt, high-resolution displays, or high-power charging, use a cable whose manufacturer explicitly supports the intended mode at the length you are buying. Active cables can extend some high-speed modes but may have different compatibility rules.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Buying checklist</p>
<ul class="article-list">
<li>Required charging wattage/current</li>
<li>Required USB data rate</li>
<li>DisplayPort/video requirement</li>
<li>USB4 or Thunderbolt requirement</li>
<li>Connector orientation: USB-C to C, C to A, etc.</li>
<li>Required length at the desired data/video mode</li>
<li>Reputable manufacturer and clear published specification/certification</li>
</ul></section>

